Docker Images

Images contain everything needed to run an application—code, libraries, and settings.
Immutable: Once created, they do not change.
Layers: Built in stacks to save space and time.
Storage: Stored locally or on Docker Hub.

Essential Image Commands
List images: docker images
Download an image: docker pull <image_name>
Delete an image: docker rmi <image_id>
Build from a Dockerfile: docker build -t <name> .

Docker Containers
Containers are the isolated environments where your code actually executes.

Ephemeral: They can be stopped, started, and deleted easily.
Isolated: They run independently from your host system.
Writable: They add a "thin layer" on top of the image to store runtime data.

Essential Container Commands
Run a new container: docker run <image_name>
List running containers: docker ps
List all containers (including stopped): docker ps -a
Stop a container: docker stop <container_id>
Remove a container: docker rm <container_id>
Enter a running container: docker exec -it <container_id> /bin/bash

